  <h1>Embedded LLMs in Game Applications</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters for <code class="inline">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs</code>: every line of dialogue your companion Wren says will come out of a model running on the player's machine. The architectural choices on this page <em>are</em> the architecture of your game.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept</h2>

<p>An <b>embedded LLM in a game</b> is a language model whose output is consumed by the running game process &mdash; usually to generate NPC dialogue, narrate events, or interpret freeform player input. "Embedded" here doesn't necessarily mean the model lives inside the game's <em>binary</em>; it means the game itself drives inference rather than the user opening a chatbot in another window. There are three ways to plug one in, and the choice shapes everything else.</p>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Style</th><th>What it is</th><th>Best when</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Cloud API</b></td><td>POST to a remote service (OpenAI, Anthropic, Inworld, Convai).</td><td>You want top quality and can require internet, payment, and trusting a third party with player prompts.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Sidecar process</b></td><td>A separate local process (Ollama, LM Studio, llamafile) hosts the model and exposes HTTP on <code class="inline">localhost</code>.</td><td>You want fully local with the smallest engineering cost. This is what we picked.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>In-process library</b></td><td>The model lives in the game binary, called via a C/C++ API (<code class="inline">llama.cpp</code>, ONNX Runtime, candle, MLC-LLM).</td><td>You're ready to ship to non-technical players and own GPU detection, quantization, and threading.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Why do this at all? Pre-LLM NPCs used <b>dialogue trees</b>: finite hand-authored graphs of <em>"if player picks option B, NPC says line 47."</em> Linear in writer-hours, canned by the second playthrough. LLM-driven NPCs trade authorial control for combinatorial breadth: the same blacksmith can answer a question no writer anticipated, in voice, because the persona prompt constrains <em>how</em> he talks rather than <em>what</em> he can say. The price is loss of guarantees &mdash; an unconstrained NPC might hallucinate a quest item, break character, or wander into real-world topics. The mitigations form a ladder of complexity:</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>Persona prompt + bounded history</b> &mdash; cheapest, what we use. Just rules and recent context.</li>
  <li><b>Structured output</b> &mdash; force JSON with an allowed-action enum, validated with a grammar.</li>
  <li><b>Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)</b> &mdash; the NPC's "memory" is a searched knowledge base.</li>
</ul>

<p>Industry footprint as of mid-2026: <em>AI Dungeon</em> (2019) pioneered the pattern with cloud calls. <em>Suck Up!</em> (2023) shipped with cloud LLM NPCs. Modders shipped Skyrim and Stardew Valley LLM-NPC mods using Ollama as a local sidecar. In 2026 we now have <b>Gemma3NPC</b> &mdash; a Gemma 3 fine-tune specifically for NPC roleplay &mdash; and <b>Llama 4 Scout 9B</b> as the low-VRAM darling. No AAA title has shipped with a fully embedded local LLM yet, mainly because consumer GPU VRAM is finite and competes with the rest of the game.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <h3>Our pick for milestone 1</h3>
  <p>Ollama sidecar serving <code class="inline">qwen2.5:3b-instruct</code> &mdash; small, fast, permissive license, and the same shape we'd swap to a 9B Gemma3NPC or Llama 4 Scout later with one config-line change.</p>
</div>

<h2>2. A minimal standalone example</h2>

<p>The sidecar technique in its entirety is 15 lines. The real game just wraps this in a worker thread and a reply queue.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">"httplib.h"</span>   <span class="com">// single-header HTTP client</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">"json.hpp"</span>    <span class="com">// single-header JSON</span>
<span class="kw">using</span> nlohmann::json;

<span class="typ">int</span> main() {
    <span class="com">// 1. Build the chat body. "system" = persona; "user" = player line.</span>
    json body = {
        {<span class="str">"model"</span>, <span class="str">"qwen2.5:3b-instruct"</span>},
        {<span class="str">"stream"</span>, <span class="kw">false</span>},
        {<span class="str">"messages"</span>, {
            {{<span class="str">"role"</span>, <span class="str">"system"</span>}, {<span class="str">"content"</span>, <span class="str">"You are a gruff blacksmith. 1-2 sentences."</span>}},
            {{<span class="str">"role"</span>, <span class="str">"user"</span>},   {<span class="str">"content"</span>, <span class="str">"Have you seen any travelers today?"</span>}},
        }},
    };

    <span class="com">// 2. POST to Ollama on localhost. No auth — it's local.</span>
    httplib::Client cli(<span class="str">"localhost"</span>, <span class="num">11434</span>);
    <span class="kw">auto</span> res = cli.Post(<span class="str">"/api/chat"</span>, body.dump(), <span class="str">"application/json"</span>);

    <span class="com">// 3. Ollama replies with { "message": { "role", "content" }, ... }</span>
    <span class="kw">auto</span> parsed = json::parse(res-&gt;body);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; parsed[<span class="str">"message"</span>][<span class="str">"content"</span>].get&lt;std::string&gt;() &lt;&lt; <span class="str">"\n"</span>;
}</pre>

<p>What this example doesn't show but a real game needs:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Async</b> &mdash; that <code class="inline">cli.Post()</code> blocks for 1&ndash;3 seconds. A 60 fps game loop can't sit on it.</li>
  <li><b>History</b> &mdash; each NPC needs its own prior <code class="inline">messages[]</code> so context carries.</li>
  <li><b>Bounding</b> &mdash; history grows forever otherwise. Cap to <em>N</em> recent turns.</li>
  <li><b>Failure handling</b> &mdash; Ollama might not be running. Don't crash; surface it in character.</li>
</ul>

