<h3>Walkthrough &mdash; the heart of LlmClient.cpp</h3>

<p>This is the real function from your repo. Each comment block names the concept it implements.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="com">// SIDECAR INTEGRATION — game talks to Ollama via plain HTTP.</span>
ChatReply LlmClient::processOne(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) {
    ChatReply reply;
    reply.id = req.id;

    <span class="com">// PERSONA + HISTORY — system prompt, then prior turns, then user line.</span>
    json messages = json::array();
    <span class="kw">if</span> (!req.systemPrompt.empty()) {
        messages.push_back({{<span class="str">"role"</span>, <span class="str">"system"</span>}, {<span class="str">"content"</span>, req.systemPrompt}});
    }
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">const auto</span>&amp; turn : req.history) {
        messages.push_back({{<span class="str">"role"</span>, turn.role}, {<span class="str">"content"</span>, turn.content}});
    }
    messages.push_back({{<span class="str">"role"</span>, <span class="str">"user"</span>}, {<span class="str">"content"</span>, req.userMessage}});

    <span class="com">// stream=false → one reply at the end. Switch to true for streaming.</span>
    json body = {
        {<span class="str">"model"</span>, config_.model},
        {<span class="str">"messages"</span>, messages},
        {<span class="str">"stream"</span>, <span class="kw">false</span>},
        {<span class="str">"options"</span>, {{<span class="str">"temperature"</span>, config_.temperature}}},
    };

    <span class="com">// FAILURE-FRIENDLY TRANSPORT — bounded timeouts so a hung Ollama</span>
    <span class="com">// can't freeze the worker thread forever.</span>
    httplib::Client cli(config_.host, config_.port);
    cli.set_read_timeout(config_.requestTimeoutSeconds, <span class="num">0</span>);
    cli.set_connection_timeout(<span class="num">5</span>, <span class="num">0</span>);

    <span class="kw">auto</span> res = cli.Post(<span class="str">"/api/chat"</span>, body.dump(), <span class="str">"application/json"</span>);
    <span class="kw">if</span> (!res) {
        reply.errorMessage = <span class="str">"could not reach Ollama"</span>;
        <span class="kw">return</span> reply;  <span class="com">// reply.ok stays false; UI handles it</span>
    }
    <span class="com">// ... parse res-&gt;body into reply.content ...</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> reply;
}</pre>

<h3>Walkthrough &mdash; the async drain pattern</h3>

<p>This is what lets a 60 fps game loop talk to a model that takes seconds to reply. Submit from the main thread, drain from the main thread, never block.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="com">// MAIN THREAD, game loop frame N: submit and move on.</span>
companion.ask(<span class="str">"Have you seen any travelers?"</span>);
ui.setThinking(<span class="kw">true</span>);

<span class="com">// MAIN THREAD, every frame after: empty whatever has landed.</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">const auto</span>&amp; reply : client.drainReplies()) {
    <span class="kw">auto</span> text = companion.onReplyArrived(reply);
    <span class="kw">if</span> (text) ui.appendLine({Kind::Npc, <span class="str">"Wren"</span>, *text});
    ui.setThinking(<span class="kw">false</span>);
}</pre>

<p>And the worker, the classic condition-variable consumer:</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="kw">void</span> LlmClient::workerLoop() {
    <span class="kw">while</span> (<span class="kw">true</span>) {
        ChatRequest req;
        {
            std::unique_lock&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(requestMutex_);
            requestCv_.wait(lock, [<span class="kw">this</span>] {
                <span class="kw">return</span> stop_.load() || !requests_.empty();
            });
            <span class="kw">if</span> (stop_.load() &amp;&amp; requests_.empty()) <span class="kw">return</span>;
            req = std::move(requests_.front());
            requests_.pop();
        }
        ChatReply reply = processOne(req);  <span class="com">// the slow part</span>
        {
            std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(replyMutex_);
            replies_.push_back(std::move(reply));
        }
    }
}</pre>

<p>Two separate mutexes (one for the request queue, one for the reply list) is intentional: producer and consumer never contend, and the main thread can drain replies without ever blocking the worker mid-call.</p>

<h2>4. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3>Sidecar process vs. embedded library</h3>
<p>People hear "embedded LLM" and assume the model is linked into the binary. It might be (in-process), or it might be a separate program speaking HTTP (sidecar). Both qualify as "embedded in a game" the way we use the term &mdash; what matters is that <em>your code</em> drives inference. The line between them is which process holds the model in RAM.</p>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Sidecar (Ollama, what we use)</th><th>In-process (llama.cpp linked in)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Who hosts the model?</td><td>A separate process you started</td><td>Your game's own process</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Player install</td><td>They must install Ollama too</td><td>One executable, drop in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Switch models</td><td>One config line</td><td>Recompile or runtime model loader</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Latency floor</td><td>+localhost HTTP overhead (low)</td><td>Function call (lowest)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Engineering cost</td><td>Vendor cpp-httplib, you're done</td><td>Own GPU/CUDA/quantization wiring</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Concurrency vs. parallelism</h3>
<p>Our worker thread gives us <b>concurrency</b> &mdash; the game loop and the LLM call are <em>interleaved in time</em>, both making progress &mdash; even on a single core. We get <b>parallelism</b> &mdash; <em>literally executing simultaneously</em> &mdash; only because modern CPUs have multiple cores. The architecture would work fine on a single-core machine; it would just be slower. Don't conflate "I added a thread" with "now it runs in parallel." You added concurrency; whether it's parallel depends on the hardware.</p>

<h3>System prompt vs. RAG memory</h3>
<p>Both supply context to the model. The <b>system prompt</b> is fixed identity ("you are Wren, a curious traveler"). <b>Retrieval-augmented memory</b> is dynamic recall ("look up what Wren learned about the player last session, paste the relevant bits into this turn's prompt"). Confusing the two is what produces NPCs that endlessly re-explain their own backstory: that's identity, it goes in the system prompt, not in retrieved memory.</p>

<h2>5. Quiz</h2>

<p>Click an answer; you'll see immediately whether it's right and why. The score badge in the corner tracks your progress.</p>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Why does each <code class="inline">Npc</code> keep its own conversation history rather than the shared <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> keeping one history for the whole game?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The HTTP request and JSON encoding cost are nearly identical either way — history size dominates token cost, not connection count.">
      Because keeping multiple histories saves on HTTP connection overhead.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Jim's conversation must not leak into Jane's. Per-NPC history is what gives each persona its own continuity; a shared history would mix everyone's context.">
      So Jim the pharmacist's prior turns don't leak into Jane the pedestrian's prompts.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The LlmClient is intentionally a singleton; we want exactly one chokepoint to Ollama. Per-NPC history has nothing to do with that.">
      So we can have multiple <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> instances running in parallel.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The model is stateless across requests; it never 'remembers' anything between calls. History on the client side is the only way it gets context.">
      Because the model itself already remembers previous chats with each character.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">In the minimal example, <code class="inline">cli.Post()</code> blocks for 1&ndash;3 seconds. Which two pieces of machinery in our real code prevent the game from freezing?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A frame-rate cap controls rendering speed, not whether a blocking call freezes the loop. Even at 1 fps the call would still freeze for seconds.">
      <code class="inline">setFramerateLimit(60)</code> and a smaller temperature value.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Streaming changes <em>when</em> tokens arrive, but the main thread would still be the one waiting on them. The async machinery is what actually unblocks the loop.">
      Streaming responses and a shorter system prompt.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Exactly. The worker thread does the slow Post() off the game loop; drainReplies() is non-blocking so the main thread can collect results whenever it's free.">
      A worker thread doing the POST, and a thread-safe reply queue drained each frame.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="UDP wouldn't help — the slow part is the model generating tokens, not the transport. And Ollama doesn't expose a UDP interface.">
      A UDP socket and a callback registered with the OS.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">What is the role of <code class="inline">stream: false</code> in the request body?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Temperature is set separately under <code>options</code>. <code>stream</code> doesn't affect determinism.">
      It makes the model's output deterministic.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. With stream=true Ollama returns one JSON object per token. With stream=false you get one big reply at the end — simpler to parse, fine for milestone 1.">
      It tells Ollama to return one complete reply at the end, not token-by-token.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="HTTPS vs HTTP is a separate concern (the URL scheme). Ollama on localhost is plain HTTP regardless of this flag.">
      It forces HTTPS so the reply is encrypted.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="History is in the request body's messages[] array. <code>stream</code> doesn't add or remove memory.">
      It disables the model's conversational memory.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

